   "sources": [
    {
     "claim": "J_lesson",
     "type": "established",
     "claim_text": "LESSON CARD (practical, for the viewer): what a posted photo can give away. (1) Fingerprints: Japan's National Institute of Informatics (Isao Echizen, Jan 2017) copied fingerprints from photos taken about 3 m away when the fingers were in focus and well lit. (2) Location: photo files can carry GPS coordinates (EXIF); the FBI warns that posted images can reveal a 'pattern of life', and the U.S. Army warns embedded photo data can tip off criminals. (3) Reflections: in 2019 a man in Tokyo found a singer's home from a station/bus stop reflected in her eyes in selfies (police account, widely reported). Rows: 'FINGERTIPS IN SHARP FOCUS', 'HIDDEN LOCATION DATA', 'REFLECTIONS IN YOUR EYES'.",
